\h Prayer of Menasheh
\toc1 The Prayer of Menasheh King of Yehudah when He was Held Captive in Bavel
\toc2 Prayer of Menasheh
\toc3 Man
\mt3 The
\mt1 Prayer of Menasheh
\mt2 King of Yehudah,
\mt3 When He Was Held Captive in Bavel
\is1 Introduction
\ip This short \bk Prayer of Menasheh\bk* (Manasseh) is presented as the prayer of Yehudah's (Judah's) king while he was held captive in Bavel (Babylon). The account in 2 Chronicles 33:10-20 tells how Menasheh humbled himself, prayed, and was restored to his kingdom, but doesn't give the words of his prayer. This document gives a prayer in his voice.
\ip The prayer moves from acknowledging God's power and mercy to a direct confession of wrongdoing. Menasheh admits that he doesn't deserve rescue, asks for forgiveness, and promises to praise God. Its central hope is that God's compassion can reach even someone who has sinned greatly.
\ip \bk The Prayer of Menasheh\bk* is recognised as Deuterocanonical Scripture by the Greek Orthodox and Russian Orthodox Churches. It is included in an appendix to the Latin Vulgate Bible.
\iot Main components of this prayer
\io1 God's power, patience, and mercy \ior 1:1-8\ior*
\io1 Confession and a plea for forgiveness \ior 1:9-13\ior*
\io1 Confidence in God's mercy and a promise of praise \ior 1:14-15\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Menasheh asks God for forgiveness
